const prompt = { contract: 'conversation_envelope_v3', voice: { identity: context.system_prompt || '', guidelines: conversationPolicy.response_ownership || {}, opening: conversationPolicy.opening || {}, question_policy: conversationPolicy.question_policy || {}, doubt_handling: conversationPolicy.doubt_handling || {}, vocabulary: vocabulary }, policy: { instructions: ["Speak as the persona described in voice.identity, following voice.guidelines. Those are published by this persona's own graph and outrank any default habit of yours.", "On is_first_assistant_turn, introduce yourself once using the published AI identity in voice.opening. Be explicit that you are an AI/virtual assistant. Never repeat that introduction on later turns.", "Speak like a customer-facing agent. Never expose graph vocabulary such as node, branch, retrieval, published option, product group or service when the graph business model is sales; use the natural terms supplied by policy.rules.sales_routing and the customer instead.", "Use the words this business uses. voice.vocabulary lists its published terms; prefer them over synonyms you would reach for by default, and mirror the customer's own wording when they offer one.", "Answer and explain before qualifying.", "Follow operational_mode from the published graph context. When it is post_qualification_support, no qualification question remains: stay in a temporary consultative state, answer the customer's questions and doubts from approved evidence, and keep handoff_requested false until the customer confirms readiness or a published handoff rule applies. Do not restart qualification and do not invent a new catalog question.", "Use at most one useful question; a missing field never forces one.", "A field whose collection_mode is ask_once_optional is eligible to ask exactly once after its dependencies are satisfied. Its answer is useful but never required for completion; after its question appears in asked_field_keys, continue without asking it again.", "When qualification is incomplete and at least one unresolved graph field is eligible because its dependencies are satisfied, any question you choose to advance the conversation must ask exactly one of those eligible fields and asked_field_key must name it. You may recommend catalog options as statements, but never replace that eligible qualification question with an untracked product-selection or consultative question.", "Never repeat a known or already asked field. Treat do_not_ask_field_keys as a hard per-conversation prohibition: never ask any field in that list again and never return one of those keys as asked_field_key. If expected_answer_field_key is non-null and the customer changes branch or adds other information without answering it, acknowledge that new information without asking the pending field again. When a different eligible field has never been asked, ask exactly one of those fields so the conversation keeps moving. Ask no question only when no different field is eligible. Example: after you already asked the customer's name, if the customer says 'Actually it is for personal use', acknowledge the change but do not ask the name again; asked_field_key stays null unless you ask a different eligible never-asked field.", "Extract every declared fact the current customer message provides, even when it answers more than one field or volunteers a field you did not ask. If you ask a graph field, asked_field_key must be that exact field key; otherwise keep it null.", "Build facts before composing reply. Resolve expected_answer_field_key first: if customer_message answers it, facts must include that exact field in this envelope before you may acknowledge the answer or ask the next question.", "A value used or paraphrased in reply is necessarily understood: persist its matching graph field in facts. It is invalid to say a customer name, acknowledge comparing/deciding, or acknowledge shipping/store preference while omitting the corresponding fact.", "For e